<section class="shell legal sh-guide" aria-labelledby="sh-guide-title">
  <h2 id="sh-guide-title">How to use this shift handover template</h2>
  <p>A shift handover, also called a shift handoff or, in many plants and control rooms, a shift turnover, is the few minutes in which one shift passes the state of the work to the next. This page is a structured template for it: you fill in the five parts of the form, and the sheet below it becomes the handover the next shift reads.</p>

  <h3>Choose a template and its numbers</h3>
  <p>Pick the type of work: warehouse, hotel front office or delivery station. Each comes with six numbers to report and a handover check of six points. Any number can be renamed and you can add your own, so another kind of operation can start from the closest template and keep its own figures.</p>

  <h3>Write down open issues, each with one owner</h3>
  <p>One line per issue: what is open and what the risk is, a priority, one person on the next shift who owns it, and by when. Tick an issue once it is solved. When you close the shift, every unticked issue carries over to the next handover, which shows how many times it has been carried.</p>

  <h3>Go through the handover check together</h3>
  <p>Hand over face to face, with the sheet: it is the agenda for a short talk, not a replacement for it. Work through the six points of the check, and let the person taking over confirm the takeover once they have read it and asked their questions.</p>

  <h3>Print it, save it as a PDF or send it on</h3>
  <p>A finished handover can be printed, saved as a PDF or copied as text. To reach the next shift on another device, it can be sent as a link, a QR code or a file, sealed with a transfer code that you pass on in person or by phone. The handover is kept in this browser; nothing is uploaded.</p>

  <h3>A short example</h3>
  <p>An invented warehouse late shift, outbound, docks 1–6:</p>
  <ul class="tips">
    <li><strong>Numbers:</strong> 4,180 units handled; 320 units of backlog at handover, counted and sorted; damage 0.4%.</li>
    <li><strong>Open issue, high:</strong> the door of dock 4 does not close fully. Owner: the night shift lead, by 23:00. Carried once.</li>
    <li><strong>Heads-up:</strong> two trailers for the early wave are booked for 05:30.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety:</strong> one near miss with a pallet truck at dock 2, written down.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To see a complete one in the form, use “Load an example shift” above.</p>
</section>
